# The Ninth Card

Ruth Vanderberg writes them on the second Sunday of Advent.

She has done it, in the small square kitchen at the back of a white clapboard house on the second block of Petoskey Avenue in Charlevoix, Michigan, at the cherry-wood kitchen table her mother Grace Ellen Boelens bought at the Sears Roebuck in Traverse City in the November of 1954, since a snowy Sunday in the December of 1994 — six months and eleven days after the biopsy came back the way she and Peter had, in the small blue Buick in the parking lot of the clinic on Emmet Street in Petoskey, quietly braced for.

Ruth is eighty-four.

Peter — the Reverend Peter John Vanderberg, called to the small yellow-brick Reformed church at the corner of State and Bridge Street from a warm Sunday in the September of 1978 to a warm Sunday in the June of 2011 — has been buried in the small cemetery behind the church since a cold Thursday in the February of 2013.

She writes nine cards.

She has written nine every year, on the second Sunday of Advent, for thirty-one years, with the same green Waterman fountain pen Peter gave her in the December of 1992 wrapped in a scrap of the *Charlevoix Courier* because he had forgotten to buy paper, in the same careful cursive she was taught in the third grade at the small Christian school on Lake Street in Grand Rapids in the winter of 1948 by a Miss Ostrander who died in the spring of 1988.

Inside each card, folded once, is a check for one hundred dollars.

## Who the nine are

She keeps them, in the order Peter first wrote them out on the same second Sunday of Advent of 1994, on a single sheet of yellow legal paper tucked inside the top drawer of the walnut sideboard along the north wall of the dining room — because Peter, who was tidy about the small things and generous about the large ones, had made the list at the same table on the same afternoon and had never once suggested changing it.

Alma College, class of 1962. The Charlevoix Public Library on Clinton Street. A small hospice in a converted stone rectory on Emmet Street in Petoskey. A church camp on the west shore of Torch Lake, where she taught fifth-grade Sunday school in the summers of 1968 through 1971. A food pantry in a converted feed store on Antrim Street in East Jordan. A women's shelter on the second floor of a Queen Anne on Sixth Street in Traverse City. The Charlevoix Historical Society, in the old railroad depot on Chicago Avenue. A scholarship fund at Peter's seminary in Holland, Michigan. And the small Christian school on Lake Street in Grand Rapids where Miss Ostrander taught her to write her own name.

Nine envelopes. Nine stamps. Nine hundred dollars.

She addresses them on Saturday afternoon at the same table with the same Waterman, and mails them on Monday morning from the small red-brick post office on Bridge Street where the postmaster, Alan Petersen, has known her since the winter of 1985 and always says the same thing when she puts the stack on the counter: *Ruth, you're keeping us in business.*

## What the nine of them know about her

In each of nine databases, in nine different offices, in nine different towns, Ruth Ellen Vanderberg appears — nearly always in the same row.

*Ruth E. Vanderberg. 214 Petoskey Ave, Charlevoix, MI 49720. $100. December. Annual. Lapsed-friendly. Do not upgrade. Tier 5.*

Two of them spell her name wrong. One of them, in the March of 2013, had her on a "sympathy" segment because a well-meaning young associate misread Peter's obituary in the *Charlevoix Courier* and marked her deceased for four years — she got the *In Loving Memory* newsletter every quarter through the spring of 2017. Three of them send her the same fall appeal that opens *Dear Friend.* The seminary sent her, in the October of 2019, a small postcard on cream stock informing her that she had been "temporarily removed from the mailing list due to reduced engagement." She kept it in the drawer for a long time.

None of the nine of them knows about the other eight.

None of them knows that she is not, in the way an actual person is not, a hundred-dollar-a-year woman.

She is a nine-hundred-dollar-a-year woman, thirty-one years in a row, which is twenty-seven thousand nine hundred dollars, which is more, quietly, than the annual major-gifts pipeline of two of the nine causes on her list *combined.*

And, more importantly and more quietly and more unmissably to anyone who has ever sat at that cherry-wood table with her: she is a woman.

## What she is going to do this year

This is the last year she will write nine.

She has thought about it since a warm morning in the April of 2026, when her hand — which has held that green Waterman through thirty-one Advents and forty-two Christmas letters and one long yellow legal pad of hospice visits she made in the year after Peter died — began to shake a little on the loops of the *g* and the *y.*

She has decided, plainly, in the same careful way she decides everything, that from the December of 2027, she will write one card.

One card. One check. Two thousand dollars. To one of the nine.

The other eight, quietly, without a letter, without a warning, without a phone call any of them will ever place, will lose her forever.

She has, this Sunday, laid all nine of the current annual reports on the kitchen table beside the nine unaddressed cards, and is going to read them, one at a time, with a fresh cup of the good Constant Comment her sister Colleen used to send her from a small tea shop in East Grand Rapids.

## The one that thanked her like a person

Eight of the nine annual reports open the same way.

*Dear Friend, Because of you, we...*

The ninth — from the small hospice in the converted stone rectory on Emmet Street in Petoskey — contains, on page seven, tucked between a photograph of a garden and a pie chart of expense ratios, a hand-written note on unlined paper, folded twice, from a volunteer named Margaret Hollings who sat with Ruth's older sister Colleen for the last four days of Colleen's life in the March of 2025.

Margaret wrote it in the April afterward, in the plain warm hand of a person who has not been trained in donor stewardship and is therefore free of it, and slipped it into Ruth's report before it went out:

*Ruth — I don't know if the office will ever tell you this, but the shortbread you sent up on Thursday was the last thing Colleen ate. She held a piece of it in her hand for a long time before she took the bite. She said your name once, very softly. I thought you should know.*

Ruth read the note at the same kitchen table in the same afternoon light of a warm Wednesday in the second week of April of 2025 and cried into a dish towel her mother had embroidered with a small blue anchor in the summer of 1961 for eleven quiet minutes.

She has read it, in the sixteen months since, forty-one times.

The other eight annual reports are not going to get a card in the December of 2027.

Neither are the seven after those.

The ninth, from the small hospice in the converted rectory on Emmet Street, is.

## The moment your software will only see once

There is a version of Ruth Vanderberg the industry has been trained to see.

*$100. Annual. Small. Lapsed-friendly. Do not upgrade.*

There is a version of Ruth Vanderberg that is actually walking around Charlevoix, Michigan on the second Sunday of Advent of 2026 in a wool coat her daughter-in-law Beth mailed her from Ann Arbor in the November of 2019.

*Nine ledgers. Thirty-one years. Twenty-seven thousand nine hundred dollars. A green Waterman. A cherry-wood table. A dish towel with a small blue anchor and eleven minutes of grief in it. And one decision, being made this Sunday afternoon over a cup of Constant Comment, that will move nineteen hundred dollars a year in one direction and away from eight others — and none of the eight will ever know why.*

The whole point of what we are building at Rōmy is to see the second Ruth *before* the first Ruth is what your annual report is quietly saying goodbye to.

It is to see the nine ledgers at once. To notice that a hospice in Petoskey received a hundred dollars in the December of 2024, and that a volunteer named Margaret Hollings sat four days with a woman named Colleen Boelens in the March of 2025, and that a check for one hundred dollars from a Ruth E. Vanderberg on Petoskey Avenue in Charlevoix cleared the same hospice's bank on the twenty-second of December of 2025 — and that these three small facts, plus a postcard about "reduced engagement" the seminary sent in the October of 2019, plus an obituary for a Reverend Peter Vanderberg in the *Charlevoix Courier* of the February of 2013 that named eight of the nine causes on her list by name — belong, together, to one woman making one decision on one afternoon in the December of 2026.

## What a good listener would put on the desk on Monday

Rōmy reads the small papers.

It reads, on a warm Monday morning in the second week of December, at nine-oh-eight, an entry from the third page of the *Charlevoix Courier* of the seventeenth of March of 2025 announcing the memorial service for Colleen Marie Boelens at the small stone chapel on Emmet Street and asking that remembrances be sent to the hospice on the same street. It reads a small line on the summer 2025 newsletter of that hospice thanking, among its volunteers, a Margaret Hollings of Petoskey who has, since the September of 2019, sat with families in the last week. It reads an alumni note in the fall 2024 *Alma College Magazine* naming, under class of 1962, a Ruth Ellen Boelens Vanderberg of Charlevoix, retired parish organist. It reads Peter Vanderberg's obituary of the February of 2013 and it reads, at the bottom of the small yellow bulletin of the fifth Sunday of Ordinary Time at the yellow-brick church at State and Bridge, a line under *long-standing gifts of the parish* that includes a Waterman fountain pen given by Ruth Vanderberg to the pulpit of the church in the September of 2013.

Then, at nine-oh-nine on that same Monday morning, it puts on the desk of a twenty-nine-year-old development associate at the small hospice on Emmet Street — a woman named Elena Boothby — a single sourced page.

*Ruth Ellen Vanderberg, our $100 December donor since the December of 2015, is the younger sister of Colleen Marie Boelens (d. 3/2025), whom our volunteer Margaret Hollings sat with in her last four days. Ruth received Margaret's hand-written note in our 2024 annual report in the April of 2025. She is one of nine annual $100 December donors across the region — the same list her late husband, the Reverend Peter Vanderberg (d. 2/2013), first wrote out for her on a snowy Sunday in the December of 1994 — and she is, at the age of eighty-four, quietly consolidating. Suggested: one plain cream card, this week, in your own hand — not from Development, from you — to Ruth at 214 Petoskey Avenue, mentioning Colleen by name, mentioning Margaret by name, mentioning the shortbread. No ask. No case for support. Ask, at the end, whether she would let you drive up to Charlevoix some Wednesday afternoon in January for a cup of Constant Comment at her kitchen table. Would you like a draft, in your own voice?*

Elena writes the card by hand on a Tuesday afternoon and mails it Wednesday from the post office at the corner of Mitchell and Petoskey.

Ruth reads it on a Friday afternoon at the kitchen table, in a wool cardigan Peter bought her from a small shop on Bay View in the summer of 2007, with a fresh cup of Constant Comment beside the ninth card.

She sits with it for a long quiet minute.

Then she puts the ninth card back in the drawer, un-written, for another year.

## We are not the pen

Rōmy is not the green Waterman fountain pen wrapped in a scrap of the *Charlevoix Courier* on Christmas morning of 1992.

Rōmy is not the cherry-wood table, or the small blue anchor on a dish towel from the summer of 1961, or the tea shop in East Grand Rapids where Colleen used to buy the Constant Comment, or the second Sunday of Advent, or the sixteen-months-and-forty-one-readings of a hand-written note on unlined paper.

Rōmy is the small careful page a good listener would have put on Elena Boothby's desk on a warm Monday morning in the second week of December of 2026, if a good listener had been sitting in that yellow-brick church for thirty-three years, and in the passenger seat of the small blue Buick outside the clinic on Emmet Street in the summer of 1994, and beside Colleen's bed for the last four days of March of 2025.

The whole product is a smaller, warmer job than the industry has been trying to sell for twenty years.

It is not to prospect Ruth. It is to *notice* her.

## A small assignment for the officer reading this on a Sunday

Before the second Sunday of Advent, do one small unglamorous thing this week.

Pull, out of your CRM, your ten longest-tenured hundred-dollar-a-year December donors. Not the tier ones. Not the wealth-screened ones. The *Dear Friend* ones. The ones you have been sending the same appeal to for thirty Advents in a row and have never once written back to by hand.

Read the obituary column of the local paper for the year the giving started, and for last spring.

You will find, on more Sunday afternoons than the sector has been telling you, an eighty-four-year-old widow at a cherry-wood kitchen table with a Waterman fountain pen and nine unaddressed cards and one dish towel with a small blue anchor on it, in the middle of a quiet decision that is going to move somebody nineteen hundred dollars a year for the next twenty years.

Then, from your own kitchen table, on a plain cream envelope with a real stamp — write her one page in your own hand.

Do not ask her for anything.

Say her sister's name once. Say the shortbread once. Sign it *warmly.*

Ask, at the very end, whether she would let you drive up some Wednesday afternoon in January for a cup of Constant Comment.

Mail it Tuesday.

The ninth card is on the cherry-wood table on the second Sunday of Advent, the way it has been on the second Sunday of Advent for thirty-one years.

Somebody's name is going to be on the envelope.

Turn to her. ♡
